1
00:00:00,898 --> 00:00:03,349
In an emergency, one person shouldn't have

2
00:00:03,374 --> 00:00:04,664
to make five calls.

3
00:00:05,403 --> 00:00:07,614
What if one calm conversation could coordinate

4
00:00:07,639 --> 00:00:08,821
the next steps?

5
00:00:10,698 --> 00:00:12,119
Meet Ek Awaaz.

6
00:00:12,857 --> 00:00:15,382
A voice-first assistant with a simple idea:

7
00:00:15,450 --> 00:00:16,679
let the caller speak.

8
00:00:17,395 --> 00:00:19,740
Let the system handle the coordination.

9
00:00:20,798 --> 00:00:22,232
Here's the actual app.

10
00:00:23,020 --> 00:00:25,824
A clear starting point, a live console,

11
00:00:25,880 --> 00:00:27,326
and an incident report.

12
00:00:28,044 --> 00:00:30,728
Let's walk through a simulated road accident.

13
00:00:31,498 --> 00:00:34,673
Listen, understand, confirm, coordinate.

14
00:00:35,384 --> 00:00:36,741
Here's how it works.

15
00:00:37,898 --> 00:00:40,702
The caller speaks naturally, in Hinglish.

16
00:00:41,442 --> 00:00:44,102
A bike accident, with two people injured.

17
00:00:44,819 --> 00:00:47,131
Their words appear in the live transcript.

18
00:00:47,872 --> 00:00:50,268
Key details are highlighted as they arrive.

19
00:00:50,985 --> 00:00:54,114
One conversation, turning into a shared picture.

20
00:00:57,798 --> 00:01:00,550
Beside the transcript, the incident card takes

21
00:01:00,574 --> 00:01:05,035
shape. Location, people, and severity come together.

22
00:01:05,765 --> 00:01:07,759
Watch the address update when the caller

23
00:01:07,784 --> 00:01:08,918
gives a correction.

24
00:01:09,587 --> 00:01:11,762
The latest information matters more than the

25
00:01:11,787 --> 00:01:12,740
first guess.

26
00:01:15,698 --> 00:01:17,812
The caller confirms the incident type and

27
00:01:17,837 --> 00:01:20,185
location before coordination begins.

28
00:01:20,888 --> 00:01:22,911
See that checkpoint on the timeline?

29
00:01:23,569 --> 00:01:25,664
Understanding comes before action.

30
00:01:27,597 --> 00:01:29,061
Now the work splits.

31
00:01:29,801 --> 00:01:32,001
The listener supports the conversation.

32
00:01:32,660 --> 00:01:34,912
The dispatcher handles the next steps in

33
00:01:34,937 --> 00:01:35,642
parallel.

34
00:01:36,798 --> 00:01:39,465
In this recorded demo, ambulance and family

35
00:01:39,490 --> 00:01:42,146
outreach are simulated, with a location link

36
00:01:42,171 --> 00:01:43,725
shown alongside them.

37
00:01:44,464 --> 00:01:46,759
Each step has a visible status, so

38
00:01:46,784 --> 00:01:48,574
you can follow the handoff instead of

39
00:01:48,599 --> 00:01:50,910
wondering what's happening behind the scenes.

40
00:01:52,698 --> 00:01:55,618
When speaking freely isn't possible, the interaction

41
00:01:55,643 --> 00:01:59,726
changes. Silent mode offers discreet prompts and

42
00:01:59,751 --> 00:02:01,185
keypad responses.

43
00:02:01,903 --> 00:02:03,837
Less pressure on the caller, with a

44
00:02:03,862 --> 00:02:05,729
clear path to the next step.

45
00:02:07,798 --> 00:02:10,550
The incident report brings details and timestamped

46
00:02:10,574 --> 00:02:11,732
actions together.

47
00:02:12,424 --> 00:02:14,502
This is a prototype, not a live

48
00:02:14,527 --> 00:02:16,045
emergency response.

49
00:02:16,787 --> 00:02:19,639
No public emergency service was contacted.

50
00:02:20,368 --> 00:02:22,199
Every step stays visible.

51
00:02:23,298 --> 00:02:24,444
Ek Awaaz.

52
00:02:25,171 --> 00:02:27,134
One voice, many calls.

53
00:02:27,863 --> 00:02:29,117
Built with Bolna.
